    explanation: "Retinal pigment epithelial (RPE) cells perform the critical daily phagocytosis of shed photoreceptor outer segment discs — approximately 10% of rod outer segment length is shed and replaced each day in a circadian rhythm peaking at dawn. RPE cells recognize outer segment tips via alphavbeta5 integrin and MerTK (a receptor tyrosine kinase)-mediated recognition of phosphatidylserine exposed on shed disc membranes. Following phagocytosis, the outer segment discs are degraded in phagolysosomes. ABCA4 (ATP-binding cassette subfamily A member 4) is a flippase expressed on photoreceptor disc membranes that transports all-trans-retinal (the released chromophore from photoactivated rhodopsin) from the intradiscal to the cytoplasmic leaflet, where it is reduced to all-trans-retinol by NADPH-dependent retinol dehydrogenase. Loss-of-function mutations in ABCA4 cause accumulation of bisretinoid compounds, especially N-retinylidene-N-retinylethanolamine (A2E), in RPE lysosomes. A2E is toxic, causing RPE cell death and secondary photoreceptor degeneration — it is implicated in Stargardt disease (the most common inherited macular dystrophy) and contributes to the lipofuscin accumulation and drusen formation in AMD. Muller cells (A) provide metabolic support and Müller glia scaffold. BEST1 mutations (C) cause vitelliform macular dystrophy via RPE Cl- channel dysfunction.",
