    explanation: "Secretory IgA (sIgA) production and secretion involves coordinated steps across multiple cell types. Lamina propria plasma cells (IgA-committed B cells that underwent class switching, typically in Peyer's patches and mesenteric lymph nodes under the influence of TGF-beta, IL-10, and IL-6) secrete IgA as dimers joined by the J chain (a 15 kDa polypeptide produced by the plasma cell). Dimeric IgA binds with high affinity to the polymeric immunoglobulin receptor (pIgR), a 100 kDa type I transmembrane glycoprotein expressed on the basolateral surface of intestinal epithelial cells (also hepatocytes, and mucosal cells of the respiratory and urogenital tracts). Binding triggers receptor-mediated endocytosis and transcytosis in vesicles along the actin/microtubule cytoskeleton to the apical membrane. At the apical surface, the extracellular domain of pIgR (the 'secretory component' or 'secretory piece') is cleaved by serine proteases (TMPRSS13, furin). The released sIgA complex (dimeric IgA + J chain + secretory component) enters the intestinal lumen. The secretory component protects sIgA from luminal proteolytic degradation and mediates anchoring to mucus. sIgA neutralizes pathogens/toxins and prevents microbial adherence (immune exclusion) without activating complement, avoiding inflammatory tissue damage. In IgA deficiency, patients are susceptible to recurrent mucosal infections.",
