    explanation: "Onion bulb formations (also called 'onion skin' or Bungner band-related structures) are the histopathologic hallmark of chronic hypertrophic demyelinating neuropathy. They result from repeated cycles of segmental demyelination followed by incomplete remyelination by Schwann cells. Each cycle leaves behind a layer of redundant Schwann cell cytoplasm and basal lamina around the axon, and over many cycles these layers accumulate into the characteristic onion bulb appearance. Electron microscopy confirms the concentric layers of Schwann cell processes. The most common hereditary form is CMT1A (Charcot-Marie-Tooth disease type 1A), caused by a 1.5 Mb tandem duplication on chromosome 17p12 containing the PMP22 gene (peripheral myelin protein 22) — the most common genetic neuropathy worldwide. PMP22 overexpression from the duplication causes abnormal myelin assembly and instability, triggering repeated demyelination. CMT1B is caused by mutations in MPZ (myelin protein zero, P0); CMT1X by Cx32 (connexin-32) mutations. The axons in onion bulbs are usually small-caliber, thinly myelinated, or demyelinated. Nerve conduction studies show markedly slowed velocity. Wallerian degeneration (A) is distal axon degeneration after proximal axotomy and does not cause onion bulbs. Leprosy (D) causes granulomatous nerve invasion.",
